group_by() + summarize() Side by Side
No grouping — one global row:
birds |&gt;
  summarize(total_obs = sum(how_many, na.rm = TRUE))
With grouping — one row per state:
birds |&gt;
  group_by(state) |&gt;
  summarize(total_obs = sum(how_many, na.rm = TRUE))

Speaker Notes: Run both of these live and have students predict the number of rows before you run them. The first returns one row — the global total. The second returns one row per unique state value. The only difference is the addition of group_by(). This contrast is the core demo of the day. Do not rush past it.

The NA Problem in Summarize
# One NA poisons the whole group sum:
birds |&gt;
  group_by(state) |&gt;
  summarize(
    sum_with_na    = sum(how_many),              # returns NA
    sum_without_na = sum(how_many, na.rm = TRUE) # returns a number
  )
Question: Does NA in how_many mean zero birds, or was the count not recorded?

Speaker Notes: This is a deliberate teaching moment. Run the code live and let students see the NA in the first column. Then ask the question on the slide. The answer depends on your domain knowledge. In FeederWatch data, a missing count typically means the observer didn’t submit a count — not that they saw zero birds. This is different from a true zero count. Students must be able to articulate this distinction before removing NAs. na.rm = TRUE is not automatically correct — it’s a decision that must be documented.

Handling NAs: A Decision, Not a Default
Before summarizing, filter intentionally:
birds |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(how_many), how_many &gt; 0) |&gt;
  group_by(species_code) |&gt;
  summarize(total_obs = sum(how_many))
Or use na.rm = TRUE inside the summary function and document why.

Speaker Notes: There are two defensible strategies: filter NAs out before grouping, or use na.rm = TRUE inside sum() or mean(). The filter approach is often cleaner because it makes the decision explicit and visible at the top of the pipeline. Either way, you must write a comment explaining what NA means in this context and why you chose to remove it. Silently dropping NAs without documentation is the kind of mistake that causes retractions in published research.

Ranking Results with arrange(desc())
birds |&gt;
  group_by(species_code) |&gt;
  summarize(
    total_obs = sum(how_many, na.rm = TRUE),
    n_sites   = n_distinct(site_id)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(total_obs))

arrange(desc(...)) sorts from highest to lowest.
Chain it after summarize() in the same pipe.


Speaker Notes: After we summarize, we almost always want to rank the results. arrange(desc()) is the tool for that. Point out that arrange() comes after summarize() in the pipe — not before. Order matters in a pipe: each step receives the output of the previous step.

