Anatomy of a Function
# 1. Name      2. Arguments
summarize_species &lt;- function(data, state_filter, n_top = 5) {

  # 3. Body
  result &lt;- data |&gt;
    filter(state == state_filter) |&gt;
    group_by(species_code) |&gt;
    summarize(total_obs = sum(how_many, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
    slice_max(total_obs, n = n_top)

  # 4. Return value
  return(result)
}

Speaker Notes: Walk through each numbered comment in the code. Emphasize: (1) name is how you call it — use snake_case; (2) arguments are the “knobs” the caller controls — n_top = 5 gives a default so the argument is optional; (3) the body is just the same pipeline they already know; (4) return() makes the output explicit. Ask: “What would the function return if I removed return(result)?” Answer: R returns the last evaluated expression, so it would still work — but explicit return() is a best practice for readability.

Functions Inside mutate()
# A helper function
classify_abundance &lt;- function(total) {
  if (total &gt;= 500) return("High")
  if (total &gt;= 100) return("Medium")
  return("Low")
}

# Apply it to every row via map_chr() inside mutate()
all_results |&gt;
  mutate(abundance_class = map_chr(total_obs, classify_abundance))

Speaker Notes: Point out that classify_abundance() works on a single number, not a whole vector. That is why we need map_chr() inside mutate() — it applies the function row-by-row and collects the character results. Ask: “Why map_chr and not map?” Answer: map() returns a list; map_chr() returns a character vector, which is what mutate() expects for a new column. If students are fuzzy on this, connect it to Week 12’s map_dbl() and map_chr() examples.

